re are certain tenants  it's a different story a different world  a different moral a different narrative  but probably have horses it's probably  have cowboys might be some Native  Americans probably be a saloon there  might be a bank there might be  stagecoach there are just certain things  good guys bad guys you know it's it's it  was a little bit like needing to know  okay if we're if we're telling a story  in this genre in this Star Wars genre  what are certain things that feel like  essential elements but once we had those  things everything was serving the story  who would these new characters who are  we meeting we've never met before what  is their story why is it relevant why  are we telling a story at all in this in  this universe and that was I think the  most fun we had what did you how did you  go about ensuring that the movie would  be relatable to a whole new generation  of fans well the the key to this movie  was not any of the accoutrements or  props or ships or locales it was all  about who is the story about and when  Larry Kasdan and I began writing the  script that was the fundamental question  we would make ourselves tell each other  the story you know this is a story about  a young woman who this story about a  young man who like tell the story and  it's amazing how that is sort of a  litmus test for something that either  works or not because you can't say it's  a story about really cool gadgets it's a  story about spaceships that flood none  of that will work it's a story about  someone who it's it's about the heart  and soul of the characters so that was  the thing that we focused on trying to  figure out what makes the story  compelling for us and hope that if we  can look each other in the eye and say I  love that that really works that's  that's getting me excited then other  people might feel it talked about the  importance of music in the film when I  look at the list of things um when I  think of the things that that George  Lucas did and and got right got  perfectly in that first movie it is so  long and stunning to think of of those  things but nothing on that list is more  important than the music that's not to  say there aren't things that are as  important but it's not more important  and what John Williams did his gift to  Star Wars and to fans  it is you can't oversell the importance  of what he does and to know that he was  coming back to create a score for the  force awakens was as exciting as any  other aspect of working on this movie  from the very first discussion I had  with Kathy Kennedy to be on the stage  when John Williams conducted the main  titles which of course is familiar to  everyone who knows Star Wars that was  incredible but then to watch him conduct  and hear the orchestra play so  beautifully the end title is the almost  eight minute long end titles what was  incredible about that was it starts the  way it always starts but then it goes  into it dovetails into these themes  Ray's theme the resistance theme kylo  ren's theme these incredible cues that  we had become accustomed to over the  months of doing post-production that  were so beautiful and soaring and  emotional and powerful and somehow  perfectly integrated with the score and  the music that we know the forest theme  latest thing he just like no one else is  able to take what we know and do  something brand new and somehow allow  them to co-exist and I realize that's  what we were trying to do all along  you

